</svg></div><div class="max-width-110"><div class="markers-modules_tab-link-title">Body Mass Intex</div></div></a></div></div></div></div></section><section class="section markers-modules"><div class="container-large is-reducer"><div class="markers-modules_component"><div data-current="Tab 1" data-easing="ease" data-duration-in="300" data-duration-out="100" class="markers-modules_tabs w-tabs"><div class="markers-modules_tabs-content w-tab-content"><div data-w-tab="Tab 1" id="Blood-Oxygen" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane w--tab-active"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Vascular Age</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">Vascular age compares the health of your blood vessels to your biological age, which can help to evaluate someone’s cardiovascular condition over time. It considers the age of your arteries both big and small. Diagnosing vascular age involves assessing artery stiffness.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 2" id="Cardiac-workload" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Wellness score</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">A wellness score is a single, easy-to-read score summarizing overall health based on key physiological markers. </p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 3" id="Atherosclerotic-Cardiovascular-Disease" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Waist-to-height ratio</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">While BMI can give insight into obesity-related risks, someone’s waist-to-height ratio is a more accurate predictor of such risks. This is because it considers your waist, particularly the amount of fat that sits around someone’s abdomen. This fat consists of subcutaneous fat (fat under the skin) and visceral fat (fat around the organs). Both types of fat are associated with high blood pressure, high cholesterol, and type 2 diabetes.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 4" id="Parasympathetic-Activity" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Body Fat percentage</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">While other measurements like BMI can certainly be informative, they don’t capture all aspects of weight and health. Indicators like body fat percentage can be a bit more useful, as it helps to distinguish the ratio between lean muscle and fat mass.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 5" id="Cardiovascular-Event-Risk" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Basal Metabolic Rate</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">Someone’s basal metabolic rate (BMR) is the minimum amount of calories that someone’s body requires to function. This number of calories is necessary to support bodily processes like blood circulation, breathing, and body temperature. BMR is the most important source of energy for the body, making up 60 to 70 percent of the total energy the body needs</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 6" id="Atherosclerotic-cardiovascular-disease" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Body Shape Index</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">Measures the number of heartbeats per minute, a key indicator of cardiovascular health and fitness.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 7" id="Cardiac-workload-2" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Body Roundness Index</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">Body roundness index (BRI) is a calculation that includes someone’s height, weight, and waist circumference. Like some other indicators, it gives a clearer picture as to how fat is distributed throughout one’s body. The more fat in the abdomen, the higher risk someone is for health conditions. This is because excess fat can cause chronic inflammation, resulting in conditions like diabetes, cardiovascular disease, arthritis, and cancer. In general, a waist measurement above 35 inches in women and 40 inches in men is considered unhealthy.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 8" id="Cardiovascular-Ev-2ent-Risk" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Conicity Index</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">Conicity index (CI) is another indicator of weight and overall health. Experts created the conicity index as a way to evaluate obesity and body fat distribution. To do so, the equation considers waist circumference, weight, and height.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div><div data-w-tab="Tab 9" id="Body-Mass-Intex" class="markers-modules_tab-pane w-tab-pane"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content"><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-top"><div class="markers-modules_tab-section-tag">Health indices</div><div class="text-size-tiny text-weight-semibold text-color-light-green">Based on face-scan and user input</div></div><div class="spacer-medium"></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-mid"><div class="heading-style-h4 text-weight-medium">Total Daily Energy Expendeture</div><p class="text-size-regular opacity-80">Total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) is the number of calories someone’s body uses within 24 hours. This value includes the amount of calories your body uses both at rest and during activity. Your TDEE is calculated based on your age, sex, height, weight, lean body mass, and activity level. Thus, caloric requirements can vary significantly amongst individuals.</p></div><div class="markers-modules_tab-content-graphic-wrapper"></div></div></div></div><div class="markers-modules_tabs-menu w-tab-menu"><a data-w-tab="Tab 1" class="markers-modules_tab-link w-inline-block w-tab-link w--current"><div class="markers-modules_tab-link-svg w-embed"><svg width="27" height="27" viewBox="0 0 27 27" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
